  "notes": "GAIA2 (Froger et al., 2025; arXiv:2509.17158) is an agentic benchmark built on Meta's ARE (Agents Research Environments) platform. ARE simulates a Mobile smartphone with 10 distinct 'universes' and 101 tools covering email, messaging, calendar, contacts, shopping, and similar surfaces. The core release is 800 unique human-annotated scenarios. With the Agent2Agent (160) and Noise (160) augmentations, derived from a 160-scenario Gaia2-mini stratified subset, the total expands to 1,120 scenarios. Seven capability dimensions are evaluated at 160 scenarios each: Execution, Search, Adaptability, Time, Ambiguity, Agent2Agent, and Noise.\n\nThe primary metric is pass@1. Pass/fail on each scenario is decided by the ARE Verifier, a rubric-based LLM judge running Llama 3.3 70B Instruct at temperature 0. Each scenario is run 3 times, and the final number is macro-averaged across splits. ARE is asynchronous. Environment time advances independently of the agent, which exposes latency-sensitive failure modes invisible in static benchmarks. That is why frontier reasoning models score near zero on the Time split: their inference latency violates the scenario's temporal tolerances. The paper calls this 'inverse scaling' in section 4.3.\n\nThe current live HF leaderboard is the Gaia2-CLI variant. It evaluates only the 5 core splits (no Agent2Agent, no Noise) and runs the newer 'OpenClaw' harness rather than the paper's ReAct scaffold. Post-launch leaderboard scores therefore cannot be compared directly against paper Table 2. License is MIT (GitHub). The arXiv listing is 2509.17158 v1 (2025-09-21) with v2 on 2025-12-10. The `release_date` field reflects the v1/launch-blog month."
